APPENDIX D2 - RATED CRITERIA RESPONSE FORM – HR Advisory
Instructions: Proponents are to respond to each of the following requirements in the order presented below. Unless otherwise stated, links to websites or other information external to the form will not be evaluated. Responses must not exceed twelve (12) pages (excluding resumes or professional profiles, and work or product samples requested herein) Page size must be 8½ x 11 inches, with standard margins and a minimum font size of 10 points.
Proponents will be evaluated only on the service category(ies) for which they submit a response.
Corporate Profile
Proponents are to describe their organisation, its purpose, structure and makeup. Responses should include:
Profile and history of the organisation;
Number of employees;
A list and description of the products and services you offer in support of the objectives and scope described in this service category, the range and depth of your experience with each, and identify which are your core areas of expertise or specialty;
A list of any of our requirements that fall outside your firm’s scope.
Key Personnel and Roster
Proponents are to demonstrate their capabilities and capacity to provide the required services by describing their team, including internal and external resources. The response should include:
A brief profile (no more than half a page) for each individual (maximum of 4) who would serve in a dedicated relationship or coordination role with the Bank — for example, an account manager, lead consultant, research specialist. These are the people we expect to interact with consistently, independent of any specific engagement. The profile should include: name and proposed role, years of relevant experience, areas of specialization, professional designations, certifications, and credentials.
Describe the practitioner talent your firm can draw on, internally or externally. Rather than listing names or submitting bios for all potential resources, tell us about your bench/roster: the range of expertise available, how practitioners are vetted and developed, how you ensure quality and consistency when engaging associates or contractors, and what a typical resource assignment process looks like. We are looking for confidence in your capacity and quality standards, not an exhaustive roster.
Methodology & Advisory Approach
Proponents are to describe their approach to delivering HR advisory services and managing consulting engagements. Responses should include:
The methodology used to assess organizational needs and define desired outcomes;
Approach to developing recommendations and implementation plans;
Governance model and project management approach;
Stakeholder engagement and consultation methodologies;
Change management and communication approaches, where applicable; and
Quality assurance processes.
Thought Leadership & Innovation
Proponents are to demonstrate their ability to provide strategic insights, innovative solutions, and leading practices for the requirements listed in section 4.1 of the RFP. Responses should include:
Examples of innovative solutions or approaches implemented for clients;
Experience applying research, data analytics, benchmarking, or emerging HR practices to address client challenges;
Methods used to remain current with HR trends and industry developments;
Examples of recommendations that resulted in meaningful organizational improvements; and
Two (2) samples of published articles, white papers, research, frameworks, or thought leadership materials developed by the Proponent (maximum limit of 2 pages per sample).
Relationship Management
Proponents are to describe their approach to establishing, maintaining, and strengthening client relationships throughout the term of the Contract. The response should include:
Account management structure and governance processes;
Key client-facing roles and responsibilities;
Communication and reporting practices;
Approach to managing client expectations and competing priorities;
Escalation and issue-resolution procedures;
Methods used to monitor client satisfaction and obtain feedback; and
An example of how the Proponent successfully responded to changing client requirements or resolved a significant client issue.
Value-added
Proponents are to describe any additional, value-added services, tools, resources, or capabilities that would enhance the Bank's ability to achieve its HR objectives.  For each value-added service, explain:
The benefit to the Bank;
How it supports HR advisory objectives; and
Any conditions or limitations
Relevant Experience and References
Proponents are to demonstrate experience providing strategic HR advisory services to organizations of similar size, complexity, and operating environment. Provide details for two (2) engagements completed within the last two (2) years, including:
Client organization (or anonymized description if confidentiality applies);
Organizational size and sector;
HR challenge or objective;
Scope of services provided;
Key deliverables;
Stakeholders involved;
Duration of the engagement;
Outcomes achieved and benefits realized; and
Client reference, including contact name, title, email address, and telephone number.

APPENDIX D1 - RATED CRITERIA RESPONSE FORM – Learning and Development programs and assessments
Instructions: Proponents are to respond to each of the following requirements in the order presented below. Unless otherwise stated, links to websites or other information external to the form will not be evaluated. Responses must not exceed 15 pages (excluding resumes or professional profiles, and work or product samples requested herein). Page size must be 8½ x 11 inches, with standard margins and a minimum font size of 10 points.
Proponents will be evaluated only on the service category(ies) for which they submit a response.
Corporate Profile
Proponents are to describe their organisation, its purpose, structure and makeup. Responses should include:
Profile and history of the organisation;
Number of employees;
A list and description of the off the shelf or custom products and services you offer in support of the objectives and scope described in this service category, the range and depth of your experience with each, and identify which are your core areas of expertise or specialty;
Key Personnel and Roster
Proponents are to demonstrate their capabilities and capacity to provide the required services by describing their team, including internal and external resources. The response should include:
A brief profile (no more than half a page) for each individual (maximum of 4) who would serve in a dedicated relationship or coordination role with the Bank — for example, an account manager, instructional designer or project manager. These are the people we expect to interact with consistently, independent of any specific engagement. The profile should include: name and proposed role, years of relevant experience, areas of specialization, professional designations, certifications, and credentials.
Describe the practitioner talent your firm can draw on, internally or externally. Rather than listing names or submitting bios for all potential resources, tell us about your bench/roster: the range of expertise available, how practitioners are vetted and developed, how you ensure quality and consistency when engaging associates or contractors, and what a typical resource assignment process looks like. We are looking for confidence in your capacity and quality standards, not an exhaustive roster.
Curriculum & Program Design Capability
Proponents are to describe their approach and experience in designing, developing, delivering and updating learning solutions. Responses should include:
Description of the focus and scope of your off-the-shelf program offering. This could include programs, workshops, toolkits, resources, digital tools or platforms, and so on. Responses should include:
A list of the categories of off-the-shelf products you offer;
An overview of the quantity and types of products in each category;
How these products are accessed, e.g., subscription, licensing, pay-per-use, etc.;
A description of any models and frameworks that are foundational to your organization’s products, approach and services;
Where applicable, identify the key assessments, tools, or frameworks your firm uses. Rather than a comprehensive inventory, focus on your go-to instruments and why you chose them;
The methodology used to analyze learning needs and identify competency gaps;
The approach used to design curriculum frameworks, learning pathways, and development programs;
Experience creating customized learning solutions aligned to organizational competencies and business objectives;
Experience developing blended learning solutions, including instructor-led, virtual, self-paced, and experiential learning components;
What distinguishes your organization’s approach and capabilities, with an emphasis on learning transfer, behaviour change, participant experience and aligning with client needs and outcomes;
Examples of innovative learning practices incorporated into previous engagements.
Measurement Approach
Proponents are to describe their approach to assessing participant needs, measuring learning effectiveness, and evaluating outcomes. The response should include:
Methods used to measure participant engagement, knowledge acquisition, skill development, and behaviour change;
Frameworks used to evaluate learning outcomes and return on investment;
One (1) example of reporting provided to clients
Relationship management
Proponents are to describe their approach to establishing, maintaining, and growing effective client relationships throughout the contract term. The response should include:
Governance and account management structure;
Roles and responsibilities of key client-facing personnel;
Approach to stakeholder engagement and communication;
Escalation and issue-resolution processes;
Methods used to monitor client satisfaction and gather feedback;
Approach to managing multiple concurrent assignments and changing priorities;
Example of how the Proponent successfully resolved a significant client issue or adapted to changing client requirements.
Value-add
Proponents are to describe any additional, value-added services, tools, resources, or capabilities that would enhance the Bank's ability to achieve its HR objectives.  For each value-added service, explain:
The benefit to the Bank;
How it supports learning and development objectives; and
Any conditions or limitations
Relevant Experience and References
Proponents are to provide two (2) examples of relevant projects completed within the last two (2) years, and accompanying references, including:
Client organization (or anonymized description if confidentiality applies);
Industry and organizational size;
Learning and development objectives;
Services provided by the Proponent;
Target audience (e.g., executives, people leaders, individual contributors);
Delivery model (e.g., in-person, virtual, blended);
Project duration and approximate number of participants;
Challenges encountered and how they were addressed;
Measurable outcomes achieved; and
Client reference, including contact name, title, email address, and telephone number.

The proponent is deemed to have read and accepted all addenda issued by the Bank of Canada prior to the final date for issuing addenda. The onus remains on the proponent to make any necessary amendments to its proposal based on the addenda. The proponent is requested to confirm that it has received all addenda by listing the addenda numbers or, if no addenda were issued, by writing the word “None” on the following line: ____________________________. Proponents that fail to complete this section will be deemed to have received all posted addenda.
6.	Conflict of interest
Prior to completing this portion of the Submission form, proponents should refer to the following definition of conflict of interest:
“Conflict of Interest” includes, but is not limited to, any situation or circumstance where:
in relation to the bidding process, the proponent has an unfair advantage or engages in conduct, directly or indirectly, that may give it an unfair advantage, including but not limited to:
having, or having access to, information in the preparation of its proposal that is confidential and not available to other proponents;
communicating with any person with a view to influencing preferred treatment in the RFP process;
engaging in conduct that compromises, or could be seen to compromise, the integrity of the open and competitive RFP process and render that process non-competitive and unfair;
in relation to the performance of its contractual obligations contemplated in the contract that is the subject of this procurement, the proponent’s other commitments, relationships or financial interests could, or could be seen to:
exercise an improper influence over the objective, unbiased and impartial exercise of the proponent’s independent judgment;
compromise, impair or be incompatible with the effective performance of the proponent’s contractual obligations.
Proponents must clearly state in their proposal that:
there was no conflict of interest in preparing its proposal; and
there is no foreseeable conflict of interest in performing the contractual obligations contemplated in the RFP;
OR, if there is a conflict of interest, proponents must provide full details on the nature of the conflict of interest and any steps taken to mitigate it. If a proposal does not declare a conflict of interest, then the proponent will be deemed to have no conflict of interest with respect to the submission of a proposal or the performance of a potential contract.

